Z||000011||fierospeeder||07-09-2000||12:19 AM||||You can buy one of those portable air tanks that you refill with air from your compressor.  Somehow mount it in your trunk and run a line to the intake.  <P>The problem might be that the air tank will be to small?  Is it possible to run pure oxygen (compressed)through the intake, like nitrous sort of does?  Or will it be to explosive?||||reg||1
Z||000012||olympic||07-09-2000||03:28 AM||||  Ok, here's the scoop.  You cant use the compressed air idea.  Think about it this way.  You run a air hose from the pressure tank to your throttle body or air box and open a valve.  Sure the air will spray into your intake but it wont create any boost.  Any excess pressure will flow the other direction back through your air filter.  Even if you could stop this reverse flow with some sort of valve, it would take a huge air tank to hold enough air to supply an engine for even a 1/4 mile race.<P>   As for the pure oxygen idea, if it was safe they would do that instead of nitrous.  Pure O2 is extremely flammable and explosive.  You might as well have a case on TNT in the trunk.  Pure O2 also burns way too hot and would melt your engine.  Years ago I had a 125cc Kawasaki and I took my dads oxy/acetylene torch and sprayed pure O2 into the air box.  The engine rpm's went straight to redline and it scared the hell out of me.  I didn't even touch the throttle.<BR>   The reason nitrous is used is because it is quite stable and safe to carry around.  It will only burn when heated to 800*C(like in the combustion chamber).  When it burns, the 2 nitrogen atoms split from the 1 oxygen atom and the oxygen is then free to combust with the fuel and increase HP.  The nitrogen doesn't really do anything.  Another advantage to N2O is that is changes from a liquid to a gas when sprayed into the intake.  This cools the intake charge and helps prevent detonation.<P>------------------<BR>"Born free....taxed to death."||||reg||1
